The framebuffer's physical address must not be in fix.smem_start

The DRM's own framebuffer helper deliberately does not put the physical
address of the framebuffer in fix.smem_start. It only does so under
CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_LEAK_PHYS_SMEM, and then only if the field is still
zero:

    /* Shamelessly leak the physical address to user-space */
    #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_LEAK_PHYS_SMEM)
            if (drm_leak_fbdev_smem && fbi->fix.smem_start == 0)
                    fbi->fix.smem_start =
                            page_to_phys(virt_to_page(fbi->screen_buffer));
    #endif

That is not squeamishness. The X server's fbdev driver treats
fix.smem_start as an OFFSET INTO ITS OWN MAPPING of /dev/fb0, not as a
physical address:

    fPtr->fboff = (unsigned long) fPtr->fix.smem_start & ~PAGE_MASK;
    fPtr->fbmem_len = (fPtr->fboff + fPtr->fix.smem_len + ~PAGE_MASK)
                      & PAGE_MASK;
    ...
    fPtr->fbstart = fPtr->fbmem + fPtr->fboff;

so with the physical address in that field, the X server's screen pixmap
landed 0x77ff0000 bytes - two gigabytes - past the start of its
mapping, and everything it drew went there instead of into the
framebuffer. The display showed a stale picture, and the parts of it
the X server had drawn were moire'd, because what was there was the
remains of something else.

With this field left at zero, the X server's screen pixmap is the
framebuffer, and the desktop and its windows appear.

This is the same field the DRM fills in with zero, so nothing else
depends on it.
diff --git a/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c b/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c
--- a/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c
+++ b/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c
@@ -60,7 +60,33 @@
